---
title: Sex, structural size, and pre-breeding isotope variation in Palmer Penguins
description: A paper-informed replication and focused sensitivity analysis across three Pygoscelis species.
---

```python exec hide
import pandas as pd

audit = pd.read_csv("results/audit.csv")
dimorphism = pd.read_csv("results/dimorphism.csv")
models = pd.read_csv("results/isotope_models.csv")
sensitivity = pd.read_csv("results/sensitivity.csv")

body_mass = dimorphism.loc[dimorphism["metric"].eq("body_mass_g")]
body_effects = body_mass.groupby("species")["dimorphism_percent"].first()
body_min = body_effects.min()
body_max = body_effects.max()

baseline = sensitivity.loc[sensitivity["universe"].eq("baseline")].set_index(
    ["species", "isotope"]
)
adelie_carbon = baseline.loc[("Adelie", "delta_13c")]
chinstrap_carbon = baseline.loc[("Chinstrap", "delta_13c")]
gentoo_carbon = baseline.loc[("Gentoo", "delta_13c")]

candidate_sensitivities = sensitivity.loc[~sensitivity["universe"].eq("joint_model")]
nitrogen = candidate_sensitivities.loc[candidate_sensitivities["isotope"].eq("delta_15n")]
nitrogen_sex_wins = (nitrogen["sex_minus_size_weight"] > 0).sum()
nitrogen_comparisons = len(nitrogen)
gentoo_carbon_sensitivity = candidate_sensitivities.loc[
    candidate_sensitivities["species"].eq("Gentoo")
    & candidate_sensitivities["isotope"].eq("delta_13c")
]
gentoo_size_wins = (gentoo_carbon_sensitivity["sex_minus_size_weight"] < 0).sum()
gentoo_comparisons = len(gentoo_carbon_sensitivity)
excluded_row = audit.loc[audit["species"].eq("Adelie"), "excluded_outlier_row_id"].iloc[0]
```

# Abstract

We asked whether sex or structural size better explains pre-breeding red-blood-cell isotope variation in 344 Palmer Penguins. In the eligible baseline sample, males were `round(body_min, 1)`{python exec}% to `round(body_max, 1)`{python exec}% heavier than females across species. Information-theoretic model comparisons favored sex over size for nitrogen variation, while carbon patterns were species dependent: year led for Adelie and Chinstrap, and size plus year led for Gentoo. Seven focused one-at-a-time sensitivity analyses preserved the sex-over-size result for nitrogen in `nitrogen_sex_wins`{python exec} of `nitrogen_comparisons`{python exec} candidate-set comparisons. These results distinguish robust sexual differentiation in trophic-position signals from more heterogeneous carbon-source patterns.

# Introduction

Sexual size dimorphism may accompany ecological niche differentiation when males and females differ in prey or habitat use. Gorman et al. studied this question with structural measurements and carbon (δ¹³C) and nitrogen (δ¹⁵N) stable-isotope signatures in three congeneric Antarctic penguins ([doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0090081](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0090081)). We reproduce their paper-informed candidate-model approach and ask a sharper comparative question: does sex or structural size receive more support as an explanation of isotope variation?

# Data and provenance

The source comprises species-specific, citable Environmental Data Initiative packages for Adelie, Chinstrap, and Gentoo penguins. The normalized table retains all 344 observations, converts the source's `.` sex code to missing, derives year from egg date, and assigns a stable species/sample row ID. Quality flags expose missing sex, morphology, and isotope measurements rather than silently deleting rows. The ASTRA manifest records each source, methodological decision, output, and evidence link; the Snakefile and Stencila runtime traces record how declared artifacts were produced.

