# Methods

The baseline restricts eligibility to birds recorded at nests that completed a clutch and uses endpoint-specific complete cases. Within each species, we center culmen length, culmen depth, and flipper length in their original units, extract PC1, and orient it to correlate positively with flipper length. Models are fit separately by species and isotope, with female and 2007 as reference levels.

The paper-like candidate set contains null, sex, size, year, sex + year, size + year, sex × year, and size × year models. We calculate small-sample-corrected AIC, ΔAICc, Akaike weights, and full unconditional model-averaged coefficients. For Adelie δ¹³C, the deterministic paper-like rule excludes stable row `excluded_row`{python exec}: the one observation with the largest absolute externally studentized residual across the two maximal interaction candidates.

Focused universes alter one decision at a time: inclusive eligibility, shared complete cases, standardized PC1, body mass, a joint additive sex-size-year model, retained outliers, or 2,000 resamples stratified by species, year, and sex with a fixed seed. This design probes identifiable choices without implying that every Cartesian combination is scientifically useful.

# Results

Male mean body mass exceeded female mean body mass by `round(body_effects["Chinstrap"], 1)`{python exec}% in Chinstrap, `round(body_effects["Gentoo"], 1)`{python exec}% in Gentoo, and `round(body_effects["Adelie"], 1)`{python exec}% in Adelie penguins.

For δ¹⁵N, sex-containing models received substantially more baseline cumulative weight than size-containing models in all species. For δ¹³C, the best baseline model was year for Adelie (weight `round(adelie_carbon["top_weight"], 2)`{python exec}) and Chinstrap (weight `round(chinstrap_carbon["top_weight"], 2)`{python exec}), whereas Gentoo favored size + year (weight `round(gentoo_carbon["top_weight"], 2)`{python exec}). Thus, sexual differentiation was clearest for nitrogen, while carbon evidence emphasized annual conditions or structural size depending on species.

![](figures/isotopes.png)

Figure: AICc weights for the eight baseline candidate models, separately by species and isotope.

# Sensitivity analysis

Across the baseline and six alternative candidate-set universes, sex had more cumulative weight than size for δ¹⁵N in all `nitrogen_comparisons`{python exec} species-universe comparisons. Gentoo δ¹³C favored size over sex in `gentoo_size_wins`{python exec} of `gentoo_comparisons`{python exec} comparisons. Adelie and Chinstrap carbon evidence was less decisive, with the leading candidate or sex-size ordering changing under some choices. The joint model is shown separately because its single model necessarily contains both sex and size and therefore does not define competing cumulative weights.

![](figures/sensitivity.png)

Figure: Cumulative Akaike weight assigned to models containing sex or structural size across the baseline and focused universes.

# Limitations

Species and island are observationally confounded: all Gentoo records are from Biscoe and all Chinstrap records are from Dream, while Adelie records span three islands. Island effects therefore cannot be separated from species comparisons. Eligibility and missing-data rules reduce endpoint samples, isotope values integrate diet and habitat rather than directly measuring either, and AICc weights are relative to the declared candidate set. The residual exclusion operationalizes the published description reproducibly but cannot recreate an unreported visual judgment exactly.

# Reproducibility

Python 3.13 dependencies are locked by `uv.lock`. Every Python-producing workflow rule runs through `stencila execute --trace`, the executable claims above read committed CSV artifacts, and the PDF is rendered with Stencila content credentials. `astra.yaml`, the eight universe files, the Snakefile, runtime evidence, and the credentialed PDF are merged into `graph.yaml` by the repository's current Stencila graph exporter.
